00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:13.730
Thanks for having me.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:14.380
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:16.510
Can you tell us a little
bit about who you are

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.400
and what you do over at CERN?

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.910
MARIO LASSNIG: I've joined
CERN roughly 12 years ago

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:23.790
as a PhD student back then.

00:06:23.790 --> 00:06:24.790
I'm a software engineer.

00:06:24.790 --> 00:06:27.160
And I've been working
on the data management

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:29.455
part of the ATLAS experiment.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:33.190
And right now, I'm coordinating
the whole data management

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:34.960
activity for the
experiment, which

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:38.590
means the management of the
namespace, access to the files,

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:41.140
transferring
orchestrated activities

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:44.980
with a team of roughly 10
to 15 persons, distributed

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:47.170
from the institutes
in the United States

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:51.250
like Stanford Linear Accelerator
Laboratory, Brookhaven National

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:54.370
Lab, Rutherford Appleton
Laboratory in the UK,

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:58.180
also people at CERN, and then
from some other institutes

00:06:58.180 --> 00:06:59.720
that contribute a lot.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:01.660
MARK MANDEL: So for those people
who aren't familiar with CERN

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:03.118
and what CERN does,
can you give us

00:07:03.118 --> 00:07:04.732
a high level what does CERN do?

00:07:04.732 --> 00:07:06.940
MARIO LASSNIG: CERN is a
particle physics laboratory.

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.512
It was founded in 1954,
if I'm not mistaken.

00:07:09.512 --> 00:07:10.970
MARK MANDEL:
Something around then.

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:11.803
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah.

00:07:11.803 --> 00:07:14.920
[LAUGHS] The buildings look
like that, yeah, I can tell you.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.950
So what we have is a
accelerator complex,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:24.190
where we try to figure out what
the constituent processes are

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:25.810
that make up our universe.

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.720
It's kind of like where
astronomy looks back

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.670
in the history of time, we
drill down like a microscope.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:36.410
It's kind of two aspects
of the same thing.

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:39.280
So we have four experiments,
four large experiments,

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.409
attached at the Large
Hadron Collider.

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:42.450
And one of them is ATLAS.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.617
That's where I'm working on.

00:07:43.617 --> 00:07:45.283
MELANIE WARRICK: What
you're working on.

00:07:45.283 --> 00:07:47.702
And can you tell us a little
bit more about what ATLAS is?

00:07:47.702 --> 00:07:50.410
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah, so ATLAS
is general purpose particle

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:51.040
detector.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:54.670
You have to imagine that the
accelerator people are making

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.834
sure that the protons collide
in the center of the detector,

00:07:57.834 --> 00:07:59.500
and then you have the
decaying particles

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.580
going through all the
layers of the detector.

00:08:01.580 --> 00:08:03.310
We get hits in the detector.

00:08:03.310 --> 00:08:05.230
Those are read out
by the electronics.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.630
And then we try to figure
out from these hits what

00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:11.050
the decaying particles
actually were.

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:14.140
So they deposit energy in the
detector in varying amounts,

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:15.320
those kinds of things.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.290
MARK MANDEL: So what
kind of technology

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.060
do you need for this?

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:20.750
What do you do with this stuff?

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.860
MARIO LASSNIG: The actual
technology is all custom built.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.980
So those are built
upon the experiences

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.180
from the previous
detectors that came.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.720
So the original
particle detectors

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:33.580
were completely analog.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.980
And now, we move to a more
digital way of reading out

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:36.730
these electronics.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.580
You have to imagine the readout
from all the electronics

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:42.190
on the detector is roughly--
if I'm not mistaken--

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:43.545
1.5 petabytes per second.

00:08:43.545 --> 00:08:44.420
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:45.176
MARK MANDEL: Wow.

00:08:45.176 --> 00:08:47.800
MARIO LASSNIG: And then this is
then triggered down in hardware

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.520
and later on through software.

00:08:49.520 --> 00:08:53.807
And then we get event sizes,
roughly 2 to 8 megabytes

00:08:53.807 --> 00:08:55.390
in size, depending
on what's going on,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.280
if it's a proton or
[INAUDIBLE] event.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:58.780
MELANIE WARRICK:
So my understanding

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:01.625
is you're using some of the
GCP products with the data

00:09:01.625 --> 00:09:02.500
that you're managing.

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.760
What are some of the
products that you're using?

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.790
MARIO LASSNIG: We
completely integrated

00:09:06.790 --> 00:09:10.410
the Google Cloud Storage and
the Google Compute Engine.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:13.930
And the one thing that's
always missing in this picture

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.800
is that we also have
use of the G Suite.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:17.920
MARK MANDEL: Good.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:18.430
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:21.060
MARIO LASSNIG: Because really,
it's a collaborative effort.

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.520
And by making sure that
everybody has access

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:28.096
to all the documents,
slides, and everything,

00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:30.940
this is a well
[INAUDIBLE] thing.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.670
MARK MANDEL: So you said there
was 400 petabytes of data

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:34.820
per second.

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.810
Is that all going into
Google Cloud Storage?

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:41.050
MARIO LASSNIG: No, we
couldn't afford that.

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:43.300
400 petabytes of data is
what we have in total.

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:44.300
MARK MANDEL: Got it.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.680
MARIO LASSNIG: And from the
detector, roughly 20% of that

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:48.790
is detector data.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:50.620
The rest is assimilated data.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:53.026
So we have
theoretical physicists

00:09:53.026 --> 00:09:54.025
coming up with formulas.

00:09:54.025 --> 00:09:55.790
They go to the
experimental physicists.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.820
They make physics simulations.

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:00.250
And then we try to figure
out-- from the simulated data,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.710
we compare it with real
data from the detector

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:06.220
to figure out if we'd have
any excess in the histograms

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:09.960
to see if a particular
property works out or not.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.640
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.910
So in terms of
Google Cloud Storage

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:15.519
in particular, how
are you using that?

00:10:15.519 --> 00:10:18.060
MARIO LASSNIG: So we have our
internal data management system

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:22.230
that is basically tailored to
the way we have our computing

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:26.430
infrastructure built. We
have 170 data centers.

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:29.180
Those are all nationally
funded data centers.

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:31.240
So it's completely
heterogeneous.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:33.701
So our system was
built to make sure

00:10:33.701 --> 00:10:35.950
that all of these data centers
can talk to each other.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:40.380
And now, we added the Google
Cloud Platform as an extension

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:40.992
to that.

00:10:40.992 --> 00:10:42.450
So for the whole
infrastructure, it

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.490
looks like that the
Google source is just

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:46.230
another data center for us.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:50.610
And we started out with the
S3 compatibility layer of GCS.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.190
But then gradually,
due to authorization,

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.530
we moved to signed URLs.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.900
And now we can use it just
like a regular web dev

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.590
storage, which is pretty cool.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:00.420
MARK MANDEL: That is super cool.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.770
Are you just using
us for storage?

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.510
Or are you computing
stuff on us as well?

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:05.130
I think you said
you were using GCE?

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:05.921
MARIO LASSNIG: Yes.

00:11:05.921 --> 00:11:08.910
So from the workflow
management point of view,

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.930
we are operating on a
slightly different scale.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.900
So we have fewer users.

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.910
We roughly have 2,000 users.

00:11:14.910 --> 00:11:17.940
But if one user pushes a
button and launches a job,

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.850
that job potentially
has to work through 50,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:22.160
60 petabytes of data.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.450
And this is all split up
into the smallest of jobs

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:25.920
across all these data centers.

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.920
And especially before
physics conferences,

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.990
you have contention.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.140
So they will try to get
their histograms done

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.220
for their papers.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:41.310
And that is where we then
spillover compute to GCE.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:45.140
So we can fire up
VMs on GCE, ship

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:49.450
data that is needed from our
internal data centers into GCS,

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:52.214
do the computation, and then
shut it down when we're done.

00:11:52.214 --> 00:11:53.130
MELANIE WARRICK: NIce.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:56.169
And you're using
Kubernetes too as well?

00:11:56.169 --> 00:11:56.960
MARIO LASSNIG: Yes.

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:00.900
So the CERN data center
is Kubernetes enabled now.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:03.480
And in fact, one of the
things I saw at Next now

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.900
that was announced was
the Kubernetes on-premise.

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.480
So we're getting
in touch with the--

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:09.970
MELANIE WARRICK: A lot of
people are excited about that.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:11.160
MARIO LASSNIG: Yes,
this is super cool.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.120
MELANIE WARRICK: What
specifically are you

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.040
excited about regard
to that for you?

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.180
MARIO LASSNIG: From the
application point of view,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.560
this means that we can scale
out on a much easier level.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.070
Because procurement is
very much hardwired.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:26.440
So we have to make resource
requests like that.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.180
But if we can say we
scale out when we need it,

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:32.210
then this gives us
much more flexibility.

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:34.710
MELANIE WARRICK: When you were
making decisions on the tools

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:36.840
to use, what were some
of the reasons for why

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.970
you decided on GCP
or the products

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:40.474
that you specifically chose?

00:12:40.474 --> 00:12:42.390
MARIO LASSNIG: For us,
the most important part

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.280
was the openness
and the flexibility

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:45.330
that we get from that.

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:47.550
When we tried to
do this internally,

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:50.040
we came up with our own
homegrown solutions.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.160
And then suddenly, you start
to have this second order

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:57.180
effect, which means you don't
have any developers coming

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:00.060
in new into the collaboration or
leaving the collaboration that

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:02.410
actually know how
to operate this.

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:05.490
But when you focus on
tools that are basically

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:09.190
industry standard, you
open up your development

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:12.130
and engineering pool to a lot
of people who can help you out.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.470
And this is a very much
understated problem

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:15.820
that we can solve with that.

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:20.170
So from the technology itself,
it's straightforward for us.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:21.460
MARK MANDEL: That's nice.

00:13:21.460 --> 00:13:24.220
And when you built this
integrated solution,

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.914
do your scientists need to know
that they're running on GCP

00:13:26.914 --> 00:13:28.580
or they're running
on your data centers?

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.413
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah.

00:13:29.413 --> 00:13:31.690
We actually don't
want them to know.

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.980
For the user, what they
do is to make selections

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:36.230
on physics properties.

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:38.230
So eventually, they
say, give me this region

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.222
with this and that property.

00:13:40.222 --> 00:13:41.680
And then it comes
out, OK, you need

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:44.470
to run across these
one million files.

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:45.670
They're all over the place.

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.070
And then you end up with
a selection of these files

00:13:48.070 --> 00:13:50.140
on content of data centers.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:53.440
And then we just take
that part, ship it to GCS,

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:55.244
and do the computation on GCP.

00:13:55.244 --> 00:13:56.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:58.000
Any lessons learned
along the way

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.160
from building out
with these tools?

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:03.370
MARIO LASSNIG: So the
most important thing

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.700
for me personally was to
handle the authorization.

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.762
Because the scientists are
all integrated in a-- we

00:14:09.762 --> 00:14:13.900
have the US, Europe, Asia in
a single unified federated

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:15.280
authorization namespace.

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.420
And to get the credentials
from a single scientist

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:24.610
into the controlled budgeted
environment on the Google Cloud

00:14:24.610 --> 00:14:26.800
Platform, that was a bit
tricky to figure out.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:28.610
So we're happy with that.

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:31.900
For example, when we were
still using the S3 layer,

00:14:31.900 --> 00:14:36.290
we had to give out the S3
access keys to the people.

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:40.960
And suddenly, there could be
a single scientist saying,

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:42.610
OK, I need to run
the [INAUDIBLE],,

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.980
and by accident, it
starts to run on GCE.

00:14:44.980 --> 00:14:49.060
And suddenly, you rack up a bill
of things that you can't pay.

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:51.550
So that was one of the reasons
we had to really make sure

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:53.780
that the authorization
and authentication were

00:14:53.780 --> 00:14:54.380
real-- yeah.

00:14:54.380 --> 00:14:56.800
MARK MANDEL: How did
you solve that then?

00:14:56.800 --> 00:14:59.620
MARIO LASSNIG: We have
a authorization layer

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.070
built into our data
management system, Rucio.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:02.980
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.440
In terms of the research
that you're doing with ATLAS,

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:08.410
how is that research used
by the broader community?

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:09.910
MARIO LASSNIG: So
we've always tried

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:15.220
to build our data management
products on top of the computer

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:16.429
science literature.

00:15:16.429 --> 00:15:18.220
So it's not like we
went ahead and just did

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:20.297
something radically different.

00:15:20.297 --> 00:15:21.880
And this also shows
because we managed

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:24.580
to get quite a lot
of PhDs out of it

00:15:24.580 --> 00:15:27.580
with master's students working
on it who were regularly

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:32.230
publishing papers just on the
way we do our data management.

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:34.475
And this is much appreciated
in the community.

00:15:34.475 --> 00:15:36.600
On the other hand, this is
not only about research.

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:39.380
It's also of making the
product generally available.

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.920
So if you go to GitHub, you
can grab a copy of Rucio

00:15:41.920 --> 00:15:44.597
and try it out for yourself
if you have a data center.

00:15:44.597 --> 00:15:45.430
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.920
And any specific groups that
you collaborate with that you'd

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:48.490
be able to share?

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:50.323
I know you collaborate
with a lot of groups,

00:15:50.323 --> 00:15:51.790
but some that come to mind.

00:15:51.790 --> 00:15:54.010
MARIO LASSNIG: So
from the universities

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.750
we work with very closely
is the University of Oslo

00:15:56.750 --> 00:16:00.100
in Norway, Wuppertal in Germany,
and Innsbruck in Austria.

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:02.450
And then from the larger
research laboratories,

00:16:02.450 --> 00:16:05.410
it's mostly Brookhaven
National Lab in New York,

00:16:05.410 --> 00:16:07.540
and the University of
Texas at Arlington.

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:09.140
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, OK, great.

00:16:09.140 --> 00:16:10.630
MARK MANDEL: I'd love to
hear more about the GitHub

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:11.410
side of things.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:12.970
How open is this data?

00:16:12.970 --> 00:16:14.380
Or how open is this process?

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.280
Is there stuff that
people can play with or--

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:17.113
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah.

00:16:17.113 --> 00:16:20.890
So even though
this is essentially

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.110
principally a
CERN-led project, we

00:16:23.110 --> 00:16:26.359
have a completely open
Apache-licensed product.

00:16:26.359 --> 00:16:27.400
So you can just go there.

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.890
If you want to try it out,
you grab the Docker images.

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:32.080
If you find something
that you want to fix

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.080
or you want to improve, just
file a ticket on GitHub,

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:37.630
and either prod the
developers or do it yourself.

00:16:37.630 --> 00:16:38.664
And you just submit it.

00:16:38.664 --> 00:16:41.080
MARK MANDEL: And is this for
general just data processing?

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:43.900
Or is it specific
for CERN data sets?

00:16:43.900 --> 00:16:46.412
I'm guessing people don't have
access to CERN's data sets.

00:16:46.412 --> 00:16:48.620
MARIO LASSNIG: So this is
really the software itself.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:51.010
And if you have a data
management problem,

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:53.650
this is most likely a
useful solution for you.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:56.800
If you want to get access to
the experiment data itself,

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:58.900
you have to be a member
of the collaboration

00:16:58.900 --> 00:17:02.950
because what we don't want is
that people start to get access

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:05.170
to intermediary data,
and suddenly, you

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.839
get an excited
journalist proclaiming

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:11.089
some discovery that was not
confirmed by the collaboration.

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:13.554
So we have to be very careful
also with data privacy.

00:17:13.554 --> 00:17:16.339
MELANIE WARRICK: How does
somebody become a member?

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:18.400
MARIO LASSNIG: You have
to join an institute that

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:19.483
is a collaboration member.

00:17:19.483 --> 00:17:21.220
MELANIE WARRICK:
That makes sense.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.119
Well, what are some
of the future plans

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:24.140
you have for development
of your platform?

00:17:24.140 --> 00:17:25.931
MARIO LASSNIG: So our
platform is currently

00:17:25.931 --> 00:17:29.530
being evaluated by a couple of
experiments, namely, the Square

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:30.580
Kilometre Array.

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:33.120
So that's a really big
radio astronomy project.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:36.650
The Deep Underground Neutrino
Experiment, DUNE, at Fermilab.

00:17:36.650 --> 00:17:39.610
LIGO is also
evaluating and a couple

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.550
of other high energy particle
physics experiments, CMS.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.370
So what we're are trying to do
is we're trying to branch out,

00:17:45.370 --> 00:17:47.770
essentially, from ATLAS
into all these communities.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:51.550
We're trying to offer them a
service, a product, and support

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.160
community, where
everybody can contribute.

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:59.470
This means now we have to make
sure that the product itself

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:02.290
can tie into all the
intricacies and problems

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.150
that these communities
have and try

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.070
to solve them in a generic way.

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:08.200
The way the system is
built is very modular.

00:18:08.200 --> 00:18:11.740
So it's really not
specific to ATLAS itself.

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:15.340
So that's why we had very good
success in the early evaluation

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:16.900
stages with the
other experiments.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:17.680
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:18.520
MARIO LASSNIG: I'm
really happy about that.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.440
MARK MANDEL: Are other
team's inside CERN also using

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:21.220
the same platform?

00:18:21.220 --> 00:18:23.261
MARIO LASSNIG: It's
essentially a single instance

00:18:23.261 --> 00:18:24.562
that posts the whole thing.

00:18:24.562 --> 00:18:25.770
MARK MANDEL: The whole thing.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:25.910
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah.

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:26.920
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.360
MELANIE WARRICK: What are the
quirks of the science community

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:31.720
with regards to their data when
compared to a regular business

00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:32.747
enterprise?

00:18:32.747 --> 00:18:35.740
MARIO LASSNIG: [LAUGHS] They
start to get very cautious when

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.680
you mention the word
"commercial platform" because

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.980
for a scientist,
if they don't have

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.480
full control over
their data, they

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:45.410
start to get very, very anxious.

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:49.870
So it was our job to ensure that
when they want to process data,

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:54.820
when they want to store data to
ensure that even if it's moved

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:58.490
to a commercial platform
that their data is secure,

00:18:58.490 --> 00:19:02.600
but also, that they still
retain full ownership

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:04.150
and control of the data.

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:07.460
It's more of a sociological
than a technological problem.

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:10.730
But you have to make sure that
you break this mindset a bit

00:19:10.730 --> 00:19:13.790
and tell them you can trust
us, the technology is secure,

00:19:13.790 --> 00:19:15.470
and we're there
for the scientists

00:19:15.470 --> 00:19:17.360
and not for the
commercial company.

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:18.262
That's our mantra.

00:19:18.262 --> 00:19:20.720
MELANIE WARRICK: I know we're
doing this interview at Next.

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:22.370
And you were here
to speak at Next.

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:23.440
What did you talk about?

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:25.490
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah,
exactly about this topic.

00:19:25.490 --> 00:19:26.170
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, yeah?

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.669
MARIO LASSNIG: It was a shared
session, scientific computing

00:19:28.669 --> 00:19:30.020
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:32.870
The first half was from
the Pangaea Project

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:37.150
that did Earth and ocean and
atmospheric sciences modeling

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.206
on interactive notebooks.

00:19:39.206 --> 00:19:40.580
And then the second
half, I spoke

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:44.635
about the way we integrated
GCP into our ATLAS computing

00:19:44.635 --> 00:19:45.260
infrastructure.

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:45.620
MELANIE WARRICK: Cool.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:48.039
Was the interactive notebooks
Jupyter by any chance?

00:19:48.039 --> 00:19:48.830
MARIO LASSNIG: Yes.

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:48.970
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:49.040
Yeah.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:51.530
We did a podcast on the
Project Jupyter a while ago.

00:19:51.530 --> 00:19:52.924
Yeah, I think it was 122.

00:19:52.924 --> 00:19:54.590
Anyways, any exciting
things that you've

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:57.200
seen at Next while you've been
here, outside of the on-prem?

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.670
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah, I've been
speaking to a lot of people.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.150
So in fact, I've
actually spent most

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.070
of my time talking
to people personally,

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:05.970
getting in touch with
the network people.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:07.460
Because one of the things
that's very important

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:09.020
for us is to get
the data as quickly

00:20:09.020 --> 00:20:10.500
as possible from
our data centers

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:14.420
into GCS, which meant we have to
jump from the National Research

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:17.200
and Education Networks
through the peering points

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:18.200
into the Google network.

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:19.280
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:20.904
MARIO LASSNIG: If we
do that, actually,

00:20:20.904 --> 00:20:23.870
we can saturate our
data center output.

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:24.746
MARK MANDEL: How fun.

00:20:24.746 --> 00:20:26.453
Actually, so that's
an interesting point.

00:20:26.453 --> 00:20:28.490
So you peer with Google
Cloud Platform as well?

00:20:28.490 --> 00:20:29.370
MARIO LASSNIG: Yes.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:31.328
There are a couple of
peering points in the US.

00:20:31.328 --> 00:20:33.740
And the ones we tried out
was, if I'm not mistaken,

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.485
South Carolina and Brussels.

00:20:35.485 --> 00:20:36.860
MARK MANDEL: For
those people who

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:38.140
may not know what
peering is, can you

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:39.348
talk a little bit about that?

00:20:39.348 --> 00:20:42.320
MARIO LASSNIG: It's
essentially the private network

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:45.114
that is directly connected
to the Google network.

00:20:45.114 --> 00:20:46.530
MARK MANDEL: Now,
that makes a lot

00:20:46.530 --> 00:20:49.070
sense given the amount of data
that you're shifting around.

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:50.330
MARIO LASSNIG: Yeah, we
don't want to get limited

00:20:50.330 --> 00:20:51.700
by public networks.

00:20:51.700 --> 00:20:55.847
So if we get throughputs that
are 150 megabytes per second,

00:20:55.847 --> 00:20:57.180
we're going to wait a long time.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:58.730
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
cool, and understandable,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:20:59.420
like you said.

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:00.770
Well, great.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.660
