00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:53.240
MELANIE WARRICK:
So Mark, I'm not

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:54.770
familiar with Docker
or Kubernetes?

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:56.960
Where can I start
to learn about them?

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:57.830
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:21:57.830 --> 00:21:59.120
I want to talk about this
a little bit more too

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:01.430
because I think, as people
who work in technology,

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:04.280
we have a tendency to want to
talk about the new shiny thing

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:04.822
all the time.

00:22:04.822 --> 00:22:06.738
Sometimes I think it's
actually very important

00:22:06.738 --> 00:22:09.290
to go back to the foundational
aspects of what we're building

00:22:09.290 --> 00:22:11.060
on top of, because
sometimes people haven't got

00:22:11.060 --> 00:22:12.170
to the new shiny things yet.

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.461
And that's totally fair enough.

00:22:13.461 --> 00:22:16.160
So if people are like, oh,
Docker and Kubernetes, maybe

00:22:16.160 --> 00:22:18.550
this seems really complicated,
or where do I get started,

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:21.020
the ecosystem has grown so
much, I don't know where to go,

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.450
I just wanted to provide some
guidance and some resources

00:22:23.450 --> 00:22:24.180
that are available.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:25.975
So if you're thinking about
getting involved with Docker

00:22:25.975 --> 00:22:27.497
and Kubernetes,
we definitely say

00:22:27.497 --> 00:22:29.330
get started feeling
comfortable with Docker.

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:30.650
So there is Docker's
official Getting

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:32.150
Started Guide on their
website, which we'll

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:33.470
link to in the show
notes, which is great,

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:35.510
gives you great step-by-step
instructions on how

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:36.980
to get started
building containers,

00:22:36.980 --> 00:22:39.021
pushing things to registries,
that kind of stuff,

00:22:39.021 --> 00:22:42.140
just getting familiar with that
vocabulary and that toolchain.

00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:43.640
I think that's a
really great start.

00:22:43.640 --> 00:22:46.032
There's a website as
well called Katacoda.

00:22:46.032 --> 00:22:47.990
They also have a free
interactive Docker course

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:48.990
that I also really like.

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:51.029
So if you don't want
to install any tools,

00:22:51.029 --> 00:22:52.820
or just want to just
try it in the browser,

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:56.030
basically, they have a
series of all kinds of stuff.

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:57.860
They have-- oh
wow, there's a lot.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:00.140
There's, I'm going to
say, at least about 15

00:23:00.140 --> 00:23:01.880
different things
just there waiting

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:02.990
for you to play with it.

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:05.050
MELANIE WARRICK: It's nice that
it's inside the browser too.

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.920
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, if
you're like, OK, cool,

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:08.420
I'm feeling more
comfortable with Docker,

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:10.040
I want to get started
with Kubernetes,

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.510
my favorite place
honestly to start

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:13.790
is there's a comic
from the GKE team.

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:16.145
I think it's both amazing,
gorgeous, and just hilarious.

00:23:16.145 --> 00:23:18.020
MELANIE WARRICK: You
are a fan of that comic.

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:18.890
MARK MANDEL: I love this comic.

00:23:18.890 --> 00:23:19.250
MELANIE WARRICK: You do.

00:23:19.250 --> 00:23:20.360
MARK MANDEL: I think
it's so well done.

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:22.320
And I think the wonderful thing
is you can read the comic.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:24.200
It can give you some
ideas of the reasons

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.692
and the basic philosophies
behind Kubernetes.

00:23:26.692 --> 00:23:29.150
And then it just runs straight
into an interactive tutorial

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:30.380
that you can just
pick up and run with,

00:23:30.380 --> 00:23:31.504
which I think is great too.

00:23:31.504 --> 00:23:33.200
And speaking of
interactive tutorials,

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.180
also, one of my favorite
sites, Katacoda,

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:37.430
has a free interactive
Kubernetes course

00:23:37.430 --> 00:23:40.370
as well, which is just another
lovely place to get started.

00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:42.932
So it's got stuff in there
even around lodging clusters,

00:23:42.932 --> 00:23:44.640
if that's something
you might want to do,

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:47.690
but even just things like just
starting to deploy containers,

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:50.240
doing simple web apps,
just getting started

00:23:50.240 --> 00:23:52.182
with really simple
stuff to just get going

00:23:52.182 --> 00:23:54.390
and even some of the more
complicated things as well.

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:56.570
So it's a really this
way of getting going,

00:23:56.570 --> 00:23:58.700
and again, nice
safe environment.

00:23:58.700 --> 00:23:59.540
It's online.

00:23:59.540 --> 00:24:00.290
It's in a browser.

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:01.580
You don't have to
install anything

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.110
